<section class="wrap section topic" id="power">
  <h2>Power</h2>
  <p>A data center runs around the clock. <a class="cite" href="https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=28232" rel="noopener">[EIA]</a> This one will draw up to 30 megawatts at peak, <a class="cite" href="https://patch.com/new-jersey/southbrunswick/35095474/global-media-giant-confirms-it-s-behind-south-brunswick-data-center" rel="noopener">[Patch]</a> about twice what every home in South Brunswick uses on average. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-homes" id="r-homes">[note 1]</a></p>
  <p>Its 12 backup generators total about 43 megawatts. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-capacity" id="r-capacity">[note 2]</a> These are "Phase 1." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-17-russo-letter-to-township-generators-phase-1.pdf" rel="noopener">[Developer's letter]</a> Eight more are planned, 20 in all. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2025-04-23-pb-resolution-preliminary-site-plan.pdf#page=32" rel="noopener">[Planning Board, p. 32]</a></p>
  <p>New Jersey's Data Center Fair Share law "requires data centers to cut back before residential ratepayers are impacted." <a class="cite" href="https://www.nj.gov/governor/news/2026/20260707a.shtml" rel="noopener">[Governor's office]</a> Unless NJDEP restricts it, one way to cut back is to switch on the diesel generators. See <a href="#air">Air</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap section topic" id="noise">
  <h2>Noise</h2>
  <p>Data centers and their cooling run around the clock. <a class="cite" href="https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=28232" rel="noopener">[EIA]</a> The draft permit also allows daytime testing of the diesel engines, up to six at once. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-testing" id="r-testing">[note 3]</a></p>
  <p>The engines make a deep hum, with strong tones near 30 and 300 cycles per second. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-tones" id="r-tones">[note 4]</a> Low-frequency noise travels farther and passes through walls and windows. Blocking a 63-cycle hum would take a wall about 4.5 feet thick. <a class="cite" href="https://www.facilitiesdive.com/news/45-foot-thick-walls-needed-to-absorb-data-center-noise-researcher/831843/" rel="noopener">[Facilities Dive]</a> New Jersey sets separate limits for low frequencies, <a class="cite" href="{{ site.laws.noise.url }}" rel="noopener">[{{ site.laws.noise.cite }}]</a> but the noise study gives no low-frequency results. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2024-10-18-lsga-acoustical-evaluation.pdf#page=7" rel="noopener">[Noise study, pp. 2, 7]</a> Its separate octave-band letter isn't public. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2025-04-23-pb-resolution-preliminary-site-plan.pdf#page=24" rel="noopener">[Planning Board, pp. 24–25]</a></p>
  <p>With all the generators at half power, the study predicts 57 decibels at homes to the north, above the nighttime limit of 50. It never modeled them at full power. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2024-10-18-lsga-acoustical-evaluation.pdf#page=7" rel="noopener">[Noise study, p. 7]</a> Emergency generators are exempt. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2024-10-18-lsga-acoustical-evaluation.pdf#page=2" rel="noopener">[p. 2]</a> Even without them, nighttime noise at the hotels comes to 49 decibels, assuming only two of every three rooftop chillers run. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2024-10-18-lsga-acoustical-evaluation.pdf#page=5" rel="noopener">[pp. 5, 7]</a></p>
  {% for k in c.concerns %}{% if k.area == "noise" %}{% set s = c | sourceById(k.source) %}
  <p>{{ k.text }} <a class="cite" href="{{ s.url }}" rel="noopener">[{{ s.publisher }}]</a></p>
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  <p class="muted small">We've posted the noise study and are still asking for its octave-band letter. <a href="/documents/#wanted">See the records list</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap section topic" id="air">
  <h2>Air</h2>
  <div class="keyfinding" id="testing-only">
    <p><strong>Sentinel's health risk assessment counts only testing: 28 hours a year per engine, in daylight.</strong> <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-pn-dc1-risk-assessment-report.pdf#page=10" rel="noopener">[Risk report, p. 10]</a> Even so, the cancer risk from diesel soot comes in just under New Jersey's threshold: 9.86 in a million, against 10. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-pn-dc1-risk-assessment-report.pdf#page=30" rel="noopener">[p. 30]</a> That means if a million people breathed the air at the most-affected spot for a lifetime, about 10 more could get cancer.</p>
    <p>The assessment leaves out power outages and grid emergencies, which have no hourly cap. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-08-draft-air-permit-pcp250001.pdf#page=17" rel="noopener">[Draft permit, pp. 17–18]</a> The fuel tanks hold about 72 hours of emergency power, <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2025-04-23-pb-resolution-preliminary-site-plan.pdf#page=15" rel="noopener">[Planning Board, p. 15]</a> enough for all 20 planned engines at full power. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-fuel" id="r-fuel">[note 12]</a> The tank filing assumes up to 500 hours a year. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-ust-air-permit-response.pdf#page=5" rel="noopener">[Tank filing, p. 5]</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <h3>A diesel power plant in our backyard</h3>
    <p>The permit covers 12 diesel generators, about 43 megawatts. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-capacity">[note 2]</a> All 12 together may emit up to 900 pounds of nitrogen oxides (NOx) an hour, <a class="cite fn" href="#m-nox" id="r-nox">[note 5]</a> as much as a 600-megawatt coal plant, or about 14 times the average coal plant per unit of electricity. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-coal" id="r-coal">[note 6]</a></p>
    <p>NOx forms smog and worsens asthma, especially in children and older adults. Diesel exhaust causes cancer. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-health" id="r-health">[note 7]</a></p>
    <p>Only testing is capped. Emergency use has no hourly limit, including when the grid operator orders a voltage reduction. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-hours" id="r-hours">[note 8]</a> Grid emergencies tend to come in heat waves, the same days smog forms. <a class="cite" href="https://www.power-eng.com/business/policy-and-regulation/grid-operators-issue-emergency-alerts-in-response-to-early-summer-heat-wave/" rel="noopener">[Power Engineering]</a> <a class="cite" href="https://www.epa.gov/nutrientpollution/where-occurs-air" rel="noopener">[EPA]</a> The permit requires no monitoring of what comes out of the stacks. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-monitor" id="r-monitor">[note 9]</a></p>
    <p class="cta" data-until="2026-11-09"><strong>Tell NJDEP what you think by November 9.</strong> <a href="/comment/">How to comment</a> <a class="cite fn" href="#m-deadline" id="r-deadline">[note 10]</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="explainer" id="limits">
    <h3>How the testing limits were set</h3>
    <p class="lead"><strong>Sentinel knew its generators would put more formaldehyde into the air than New Jersey considers safe. NJDEP offered ways to cut the pollution. Sentinel changed its testing schedule instead, which passed on paper and does nothing when all 12 engines run in an emergency.</strong></p>
    <p>Diesel exhaust contains formaldehyde, which irritates the eyes, nose, and throat and causes cancer. <a class="cite" href="https://www.atsdr.cdc.gov/toxfaqs/tfacts111.pdf" rel="noopener">[ATSDR]</a> New Jersey sets a health threshold for how much a facility may add to the air in an hour.</p>
    <p>With all 12 generators running, formaldehyde peaked at <strong>3.7 times that threshold</strong>. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-pn-dc1-risk-assessment-report.pdf#page=39" rel="noopener">[Risk report, p. 39]</a> Levels above it reached homes and hotels up to a third of a mile away. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-05-27-njdep-draft-risk-memo.pdf#page=2" rel="noopener">[NJDEP memo, p. 2]</a> NJDEP told the company to fix it before a permit could be written: cleaner exhaust equipment, taller smokestacks, more distance from neighbors, fewer hours, or fewer engines at once. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=20" rel="noopener">[Email, p. 67]</a></p>
    <p><strong>Sentinel didn't change the equipment. It changed the testing schedule:</strong> one engine at a time, or up to six together between noon and 4 p.m. On paper, that brought the number to 0.81, just under the threshold. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=23" rel="noopener">[Email, p. 65]</a> <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=30" rel="noopener">[p. 47]</a> The schedule became the permit's testing rules.</p>
    <p><strong>The schedule covers testing only.</strong> In an outage or grid emergency, all 12 can still run at once for as long as needed, the situation that produced the 3.7. <a class="cite fn" href="#m-hours">[note 8]</a> At the company's request, "no more than six engines shall ever operate at once" became "shall be tested at once." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=24" rel="noopener">[Emails, pp. 62–64]</a></p>
    <p class="muted"><em>What this does not say:</em> a level above the threshold doesn't mean someone will get sick. It means the risk is higher than the state calls "negligible." With no emissions monitoring, the company's own logbook is the only check on the testing schedule.</p>
    <details class="more-box">
      <summary>Show the documents</summary>
      <ul class="quotes">
        <li><strong>NJDEP to Sentinel's consultant, May 27, 2026:</strong> "short-term formaldehyde risk must be addressed in order to proceed with drafting subject PCP. Please reply by 6/10/26 with a response on how to reduce the risk, including but not limited to reducing emissions via add-on controls, reduction of operating hours, limiting the number of engines run simultaneously, increasing stack height or increasing distance to property line." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=20" rel="noopener">[p. 67]</a></li>
        <li><strong>NJDEP draft risk memo, May 27, 2026:</strong> "The maximum short-term noncancer risk hazard quotient (HQ) for formaldehyde is predicted to be 3.7, exceeding NJDEP negligible noncancer risk HQ threshold of 1… The area with potential exposure exceeding the 1 HQ extend up to 500m beyond property line, including sensitive receptors – hotels, office buildings, residential houses, and an appliance store." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-05-27-njdep-draft-risk-memo.pdf" rel="noopener">[memo]</a></li>
        <li><strong>Consultant to NJDEP, June 10, 2026:</strong> "The team evaluated the needs of the few multi-engine runs and has been able to reduce the operation to a level where the acute formaldehyde risk has a hazard quotient less than 1… no more than six (6) engines operating at one time, for no more than 30 minutes within any 60-minute time-period, and between the hours of noon and 4pm." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=23" rel="noopener">[p. 65]</a></li>
        <li><strong>Consultant to NJDEP, June 11, 2026:</strong> "If the language says No more than six engines shall ever operate at once, that's still referring only to maintenance and testing operation, is that correct? Would this multi-engine run be a standing allowance in the permit since the risk is negligible, rather than a temporary condition we talked about?" NJDEP's revised wording: "No more than six engines shall be tested at once." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=24" rel="noopener">[pp. 62–63]</a></li>
        <li><strong>Consultant to NJDEP, July 7, 2026:</strong> the maximum "hazard quotient of 0.81495." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=30" rel="noopener">[p. 47]</a></li>
        <li><strong>NJDEP to the consultant, March 20, 2026:</strong> "The worst-case acute risk is all engines running at once." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=9" rel="noopener">[p. 785]</a></li>
        <li><strong>Sentinel's risk report, Table 6-7:</strong> all 12 engines at full load for one hour: formaldehyde hazard quotient 3.74; acrolein 1.62. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-pn-dc1-risk-assessment-report.pdf#page=39" rel="noopener">[p. 39]</a></li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div>

  <div class="explainer" id="cancer">
    <h3>The cancer risk: just under the line</h3>
    <p>Diesel soot causes cancer. <a class="cite" href="https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr213_E.pdf" rel="noopener">[IARC]</a> NJDEP's modeling puts the risk at 9.89 in a million, against 10, counting testing only. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-05-27-njdep-draft-risk-memo.pdf#page=6" rel="noopener">[NJDEP memo, p. 6]</a> <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-05-27-njdep-draft-risk-memo.pdf#page=4" rel="noopener">[p. 4]</a></p>
    <p><strong>Two open questions:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>All the cancer-causing pollutants together come to about 10.6 in a million. Does New Jersey require adding them? <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-pn-dc1-risk-assessment-report.pdf#page=30" rel="noopener">[Risk report, p. 30]</a></li>
      <li>At the full build-out of 20 generators: roughly 16 in a million. <a class="cite" href="#m-buildout">[how we estimated]</a></li>
    </ul>
    <details class="more-box">
      <summary>Show the documents</summary>
      <ul class="quotes">
        <li><strong>NJDEP draft risk memo, p. 6:</strong> "The maximum cancer risk was predicted to be 9.89 in-a-million (9.89E-06) for DPM." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-05-27-njdep-draft-risk-memo.pdf#page=6" rel="noopener">[memo]</a></li>
        <li><strong>NJDEP draft risk memo, p. 4:</strong> long-term emissions are the "product of PTE hourly emissions and 28 hour/yr allowed for maintenance and testing," with "daytime operations restricted to 8am-8pm (4,380 hr/yr)." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-05-27-njdep-draft-risk-memo.pdf#page=4" rel="noopener">[memo]</a></li>
        <li><strong>Sentinel's risk report, Table 6-1:</strong> diesel particulate matter, 9.86E-06 (9.86 in a million), with the other pollutants at the same location. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-pn-dc1-risk-assessment-report.pdf#page=30" rel="noopener">[p. 30]</a></li>
        <li><strong>Consultant to NJDEP, March 4, 2026:</strong> "The modeling for routine operation at the currently requested hours limit still passes." <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-key-emails-excerpt.pdf#page=6" rel="noopener">[email, full release p. 836]</a></li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>


