<section class="wrap section">
  <details class="math" id="math">
    <summary>How we calculated this</summary>
    <p class="muted small">Every number comes from a public source. Where we make an assumption, we say so. Found an error? <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}?subject={{ ('Correction: ' + site.url + page.url) | urlencode }}">Report it</a> and we'll correct it.</p>
    <ol class="notes">
      <li id="m-homes"><strong>Compared with South Brunswick's homes.</strong> The average New Jersey home uses 662 kWh a month: 662 × 12 = 7,944 kWh a year, or 7,944 ÷ 8,760 hours = 0.907 kW on average. South Brunswick has 16,770 households: 16,770 × 0.907 kW = 15.2 MW for every home in the township.
        <br>Bloomberg says the facility will peak at 30 MW: 30 ÷ 15.2 ≈ 2.0 times every home in the township. This compares the facility's peak with the homes' average, and homes only, not the township's businesses.
        <span class="src">Sources: <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/sales_revenue_price/pdf/table_5A.pdf" rel="noopener">EIA, 2024 residential data (Table 5A)</a>; <a href="https://census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/southbrunswicktownshipmiddlesexcountynewjersey/PST045223" rel="noopener">U.S. Census QuickFacts</a>; <a href="https://patch.com/new-jersey/southbrunswick/35095474/global-media-giant-confirms-it-s-behind-south-brunswick-data-center" rel="noopener">Patch, October 28, 2026</a> (Bloomberg's 30 MW). <a class="back" href="#r-homes">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-capacity"><strong>Capacity.</strong> 12 Caterpillar C175-20 engines, 5,166 horsepower each. 12 × 3,620 kW = 43,440 kW, about 43 MW. 3,620 kW is Caterpillar's <em>standby</em> rating for the C175-20 engine; its prime rating for extended running is 3,300 kW, or 39.6 MW for 12 units.
        <span class="src">Sources: <a href="{{ permit }}#page=26">draft permit (pp. 6, 26)</a>, Section A and Equipment Inventory; <a href="https://www.cat.com/en_US/products/new/power-systems/electric-power/diesel-generator-sets/1000028913.html" rel="noopener">Caterpillar C175-20 ratings</a>. <a class="back" href="#r-capacity">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-testing"><strong>Testing schedule.</strong> The permit allows testing only one engine at a time between 8 a.m. and 8 p.m. Two or more engines may be tested together only between noon and 4 p.m., for no more than 30 minutes per engine in any hour, and no more than six at once. Each engine may be tested up to 28 hours a year.
        <span class="src">Source: <a href="{{ permit }}#page=16">draft permit (p. 16)</a>, Section D, U1 OS Summary Ref. 1; OS1–OS12 Ref. 1. <a class="back" href="#r-testing">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-tones"><strong>Engine tones.</strong> The C175-20 has 20 cylinders and runs at 1,800 rpm, which is 30 revolutions per second. A four-stroke engine fires each cylinder every other revolution, so 20 ÷ 2 × 30 = 300 firings per second. That gives tones near 30 Hz and 300 Hz. The noise rule's octave bands are centered at 31.5 Hz, 63 Hz, 125 Hz, 250 Hz, and higher, so these fall in the 31.5 Hz and 250 Hz bands. This is a calculation from the engine's design, not a measurement.
        <span class="src">Sources: <a href="{{ permit }}#page=26">draft permit (p. 26)</a>, Equipment Inventory (model and speed); <a href="https://www.cat.com/en_US/products/new/power-systems/electric-power/diesel-generator-sets/1000028913.html" rel="noopener">Caterpillar C175-20</a>; <a href="{{ site.laws.noise.url }}" rel="noopener">{{ site.laws.noise.cite }}</a>. <a class="back" href="#r-tones">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-nox"><strong>Nitrogen oxides (NOx).</strong> The permit allows each engine up to 75.28 lb of NOx per hour. 75.28 × 12 = 903 lb per hour with all 12 running. This is a permit limit, the most allowed, not a measurement.
        <span class="src">Source: <a href="{{ permit }}#page=19">draft permit (p. 19)</a>, Section D, OS1–OS12 Ref. 7. <a class="back" href="#r-nox">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-coal"><strong>Coal comparison.</strong> U.S. coal plants averaged 1.5 lb of NOx per megawatt-hour (MWh) in 2017; coal has generally gotten cleaner since, which makes this comparison conservative.
        <br>Per hour: 903 lb ÷ 1.5 lb/MWh = 602 MWh, the output of a 600 MW coal plant running for one hour.
        <br>Per unit of electricity: 75.28 lb ÷ 3.62 MWh = 20.8 lb/MWh, and 20.8 ÷ 1.5 ≈ 14 times coal.
        <br><em>What this does not say:</em> these generators are meant to run far fewer hours than a coal plant. The permit's annual NOx cap is 12.65 tons, based on testing hours only, compared with roughly 285 tons for a 43 MW coal plant running all year. Diesel also emits less sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide per MWh than coal. The comparison above is about NOx, a key ingredient in smog.
        <span class="src">Sources: <a href="https://www.eia.gov/TODAYINENERGY/detail.php?id=37752" rel="noopener">U.S. Energy Information Administration</a>; <a href="{{ permit }}#page=16">draft permit (p. 16)</a>, Section D, U1 OS Summary Ref. 8. <a class="back" href="#r-coal">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-health"><strong>Health effects.</strong> EPA says nitrogen oxides "contribute to the formation of air pollutants like smog," that ground-level ozone is the main component of smog, and that ozone can worsen asthma. EPA also says breathing nitrogen dioxide can aggravate asthma, and that people with asthma, children, and older adults are at greater risk. The World Health Organization's cancer agency classifies diesel engine exhaust as a cause of cancer in humans (Group 1).
        <span class="src">Sources: <a href="https://www.epa.gov/nutrientpollution/where-occurs-air" rel="noopener">EPA, Nutrient Pollution: Air</a>; <a href="https://www.epa.gov/no2-pollution/basic-information-about-no2" rel="noopener">EPA, Basic Information about NO2</a>; <a href="https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr213_E.pdf" rel="noopener">IARC press release 213 (2012)</a>. <a class="back" href="#r-health">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-hours"><strong>When they can run.</strong> Testing is limited to 28 hours a year per engine. The permit also allows operation during power outages and whenever PJM, the regional grid operator, issues a voltage reduction, with no hourly limit on those uses. It also allows up to 30 days a year of operation when power is disrupted by construction, repair, or maintenance at the facility. Testing is barred on days with unhealthy air-quality forecasts; emergency and PJM-requested operation is not.
        <span class="src">Source: <a href="{{ permit }}#page=17">draft permit (pp. 16–18)</a>, Section D, U1 OS Summary Ref. 18 and 19; OS1–OS12 Ref. 1. <a class="back" href="#r-hours">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-monitor"><strong>No emissions monitoring.</strong> For every emission limit, the permit lists "None" as the monitoring and recordkeeping requirement. Only hours of operation and fuel deliveries are tracked. NJDEP may request testing but is not required to.
        <span class="src">Source: <a href="{{ permit }}#page=16">draft permit (pp. 8, 16–19)</a>, Section D, U1 OS Summary Ref. 1 and 3–18; OS1–OS12 Ref. 2–13; Section C, General Provisions 13 and 17. <a class="back" href="#r-monitor">↑</a></span></li>
      <li id="m-deadline"><strong>Comment deadline.</strong> NJDEP extended the comment period to November 9, 2026. Comment through the online form, or by mail to Nibras Mir, NJDEP, 401 E State Street, 2nd Floor, PO Box 420, Mail Code 401-02, Trenton, NJ 08625-0420, referencing "Draft Permit Comments for PN-DC1, PI 19525 PCP250001."
        <span class="src">Source: <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-amended-public-notice-pcp250001.pdf">amended NJDEP public notice</a>. <a class="back" href="#r-deadline">↑</a></span></li>
    <li id="m-buildout"><strong>Full build-out (our estimate).</strong> Sentinel's risk report puts the diesel particulate cancer risk from testing 12 engines at 9.86 in a million, against New Jersey's threshold of 10. The developer says these 12 are "Phase 1," and its representative told the Planning Board the full build-out is 20. If eight more identical engines were placed near the first twelve and tested on the same schedule, long-term exposure would grow roughly in proportion: 9.86 × 20 ÷ 12 ≈ 16 in a million. Where new engines go and how tall their stacks are would change this, so treat it as an estimate, not a finding.
        <span class="src">Sources: <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-pn-dc1-risk-assessment-report.pdf#page=30" rel="noopener">risk report, Table 6‑1, p. 30</a>; <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-17-russo-letter-to-township-generators-phase-1.pdf" rel="noopener">developer's letter, March 17, 2026</a>; <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2025-04-23-pb-resolution-preliminary-site-plan.pdf#page=32" rel="noopener">Planning Board resolution, ¶79, p. 32</a>.</span></li>
      <li id="m-fuel"><strong>Fuel supply (our estimate).</strong> The developer testified that eight 50,000-gallon tanks (400,000 gallons) are "sized to provide approximately 72 hours of fuel for the emergency generators," without saying for how many. Sentinel's revised emissions inventory (October 2025) lists fuel use of 245.8 gallons an hour per engine at full power. 400,000 ÷ (20 × 245.8) ≈ 81 hours, or about 73 if roughly 90% of the fuel is usable, which matches the testimony. The state registration adds a ninth, 10,000-gallon tank, for 410,000 gallons in all. For the first 12 engines, 410,000 ÷ (12 × 245.8) ≈ 139 hours, nearly six days.
        <span class="src">Sources: <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2025-04-23-pb-resolution-preliminary-site-plan.pdf#page=15">Planning Board resolution, p. 15</a>; <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2025-11-21-njdep-ust-registration-summary.pdf">NJDEP tank registration, pp. 1–5</a>; Sentinel's revised emissions inventory, Attachment B (OPRA 389866). <a class="back" href="#r-fuel">↑</a></span></li>
    </ol>
  </details>

